
[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · How should notifications actually reach people? → In-app first, email designed for (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Now the cheap-to-change-but-easy-to-get-noisy question: what counts as a change worth telling someone about. The
  failure mode here isn't missing events, it's too many — a system that notifies on everything gets muted, and then it
  notifies on nothing.

  A task app can emit far more events than anyone wants. I'd rather start with the few that reliably mean "you need to
  look at this" and add more when someone actually asks.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · Which changes should generate a notification in v1? (Pick the ones that genuinely warrant interrupting someone.)
[39m     [38;5;246m→ Assigned to you, Status change[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Assignment + status change. That's a clean v1 — both are action-triggered, so the whole system stays synchronous: no
  scheduler, no background worker, no comment model to build first. It also simplifies subscription derivation to
  creator + assignee, plus the explicit follow/mute layer.

  One more decision before I present the design. Since this is greenfield, the stack is genuinely open, and it changes
  what "an events table" and "a login" cost.

